 ## Background
-The need for adaptive grid traversal arises from the limitations of dense regular grids when handling large-scale, sparse, or multi-resolution data [G]. Early isosurface extraction methods like Marching Cubes [30-isosurface-extraction.md] assumed a uniform grid, leading to prohibitive memory usage for sparse fields. The integration of octrees with Marching Cubes [Marching Cubes with Octree](1259-marching-cubes-with-octree.md) allowed for hierarchical refinement, but required careful handling of level discontinuities to maintain mesh coherence. Subsequent developments, such as [Sparse Marching Cubes](3825-sparse-marching-cubes.md) and [Hierarchical Marching Cubes Enumeration](5223-hierarchical-marching-cubes-enumeration.md), further optimized traversal for non-uniform data distributions. In the context of Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR), methods like [2D Stitching](10249-2d-stitching.md) were developed to bridge resolution gaps without introducing topological artifacts, preserving the integrity of the isosurface across varying grid densities.
-
+The need for adaptive grid traversal arises from the limitations of dense regular grids when handling large-scale, sparse, or multi-resolution data [G]. Early isosurface extraction methods assumed a uniform grid, leading to prohibitive memory usage for sparse fields [G]. Subsequent developments optimized traversal for non-uniform data distributions and AMR boundaries [G].
